stream: count a snapshot as processed before the frame is exposed

A snapshot request waits until snapshot_requested is back at zero. The
JPEG thread exposed the frame first, which wakes the HTTP thread
(event_active on the refresher), and decremented the counter afterwards,
after the copy into the memory sink. When the HTTP thread got there
first it still saw the counter above zero and left the client waiting;
the JPEG thread then found nobody watching and exposed no further frame,
so nothing woke the HTTP thread again and the request hung until the
client gave up (the timeout in _http_send_snapshot is only checked when
a frame arrives).

It needs a moment when nobody else is watching: no stream client and no
reader of the sink. On the K1 (two cores) 1 request in 40 hung on an
idle printer and 25 in 150 with both cores busy; 4 of 8 right after
boot. The timelapse takes its frames this way, one per layer.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/src/ustreamer/stream.c b/src/ustreamer/stream.c
index 43a6131..c59e585 100644
--- a/src/ustreamer/stream.c
+++ b/src/ustreamer/stream.c
@@ -343,10 +343,16 @@ static void *_jpeg_thread(void *v_ctx) {
 			if (wr->job_failed) {
 				// pass
 			} else if (wr->job_timely) {
-				_stream_expose_jpeg(stream, job->dest);
+				// Count the snapshot as processed before the frame is exposed:
+				// exposing wakes the HTTP thread, which answers a snapshot
+				// only when the counter is back at zero. Counted afterwards,
+				// the HTTP thread can look first, leave the client waiting,
+				// and with nobody else watching no further frame comes to
+				// wake it again.
 				if (atomic_load(&stream->run->http->snapshot_requested) > 0) { // Process real snapshots
 					atomic_fetch_sub(&stream->run->http->snapshot_requested, 1);
 				}
+				_stream_expose_jpeg(stream, job->dest);
 				US_LOG_PERF("JPEG: ##### Encoded JPEG exposed; worker=%s, latency=%.3Lf",
 					wr->name, us_get_now_monotonic() - job->dest->grab_begin_ts);
 			} else {
